MATING MATERIAL IV RESUME 1.0.2
Prepared 31 October 2026, Toronto

SAVE AND RUN
Extract this folder in ~/Desktop/Lichess_Desktop, then run:
  bash "$HOME/Desktop/Lichess_Desktop/Mating_Material_IV_Resume_v1_0_2/RUN_IV_RESUME.command"

EXPECTED RUNTIME
Allow 2–4 hours provisionally for verification and the four remaining stages:
comparison chronology, cached metadata, comparison frames, and comparison
models. A fresh local chronology scan or slow external drive can extend this.
The previous fourteen stages are preserved. Startup tests take about one
minute. XT_Pro must be mounted. MatingMaterial_IV_Env_v100 is reused.
Defaults: 10 workers maximum, 11 GiB memory, one BLAS thread, at most two
heavy data jobs and sequential model fits. Chronology joins run one chunk
at a time with a 3 GB DuckDB limit. The startup free-space check is 160 GiB.
Caffeinate prevents idle sleep on macOS. Keep the Mac plugged in.

WHAT WAS FIXED
The resumed run exhausted memory while joining about 45 million chronology
rows in one query. Both the reuse join and the final eligibility join now:
  - materialize at most one million integer row IDs per range before joining;
  - verify source identity, unique IDs and exact coverage within every range;
  - include old-only ranges so missing/extra rows cannot escape validation;
  - checkpoint each completed range, with checksums and dependency checks;
  - concatenate sorted results through 65,536-row Arrow batches.
There is no full-frame hash join or full-frame sort in these two steps. New
query rows still use the original cached local chronology scanner and merger.
The prior fixed-effect solver and nested chronology-cache repairs are retained.

VALIDATION
62 batch/companion tests and 50 original Uniform90 tests passed. The new
small-data tests agree exactly with the original SQL and exercise corrupt
files, changed inputs, missing/extra/duplicate/null IDs, empty frames and
resumption after an interrupted assembly. A five-million-row synthetic check
reproduced the old out-of-memory failure at a 0.384 GB DuckDB limit; both
bounded joins completed at that same limit and passed factual-value and
eligibility checks. This synthetic timing is not a Mac production benchmark.
See VALIDATION.json and validation/ for the executed tests and optional
benchmark. The benchmark is not part of normal startup.

PRESERVING COMPLETED WORK
This repair targets the reviewed partial run 12093d55b7ee9f73 at:
  __PRIVATE_VALUE_1__/derived/replication/
  mating_material_iv_batch_v100_PRIVATE/12093d55b7ee9f73
It requires the latest fourteen-stage state from the v1.0.1 return. It will
not start a fresh full rerun if that run is absent. The baseline configuration
and original package remain unchanged. REPAIR_V102.json separately records
this execution repair and its package hash; REPAIR_V101.json is preserved.
Completed receipt and comparison reports, material inputs, frames, candidate
scores and primary estimates are authenticated. Completed stages are skipped.
Run the same command after interruption to reuse completed chunks and stages.
Checksums and dependency checks are not disabled.

The all-time-five support rule, both-role author exclusions, 90-day windows,
first-pair rules, zero-coded outcomes and shared-account-or-date CR0 covariance
remain in effect. Production uses cached local files; no game downloads, API
calls, Stockfish, rating replay or profile queries. If genuinely new comparison
games lack cached metadata, OFFLINE_HOLD records them and preserves the work.

RETURN THESE
The consolidated desktop outputs are updated after every stage:
  MATERIAL_IV_BATCH_12093d55b7ee9f73_RETURN.zip
  MATERIAL_IV_BATCH_12093d55b7ee9f73_RETURN.zip.sha256
Return the ZIP and checksum. Private Parquets remain on XT_Pro.
BATCH_COMPLETE covers these eighteen named stages, not the whole paper.
COVERAGE.json lists the remaining analyses and manuscript reconciliation.

Optional verification without starting unfinished stages:
  bash "$HOME/Desktop/Lichess_Desktop/Mating_Material_IV_Resume_v1_0_2/RUN_IV_RESUME.command" --preflight-only

The complete production repair cannot be run here because those data are on
the Mac. REVIEW_RESULTS.txt summarizes the verified return; VALIDATION.json
records what has actually been tested.
